{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Six wagons, צב.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): it is a species among the kinds of cattle that pull the wagons, as it is said (Isaiah 66:20): ״בצבים ובפרדים״. Or one may say that צב means full, from the language of (Numbers 5:21): ״ואת בטנך צבה״." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: ״שש עגלות צב״ corresponds to the six heavens, and not seven they are. Said R. Avin: that one of the King opened — said Moses before the Holy One, blessed be He: Master of all worlds! Perhaps one of the cows will die and one of the wheels will break, and the offering of the princes will be found invalid, and the service of the Mishkan will be found annulled. Immediately: ״ויאמר ה׳ אל משה לאמר קח מאתם והיו״ — He gave them הויה (existence), that they should be alive and enduring forever and ever. And how long did they endure? The Rabbis say: in the Eternal House (the Temple) they offered them, as it is said (II Chronicles 7:5): ״ויזבח המלך שלמה את זבח הבקר עשרים ושנים אלף״ — it does not say זבח בקר but rather זבח הבקר. Which cattle? You must say: the two wagons and the four cattle, and it is written: ״ואת ארבע העגלות ואת שמונת הבקר״. Rabbi Meir says: until now they endure — they were not weakened, they did not age, they were not torn, but they are alive and enduring. And this is a ק״ו (kal va-homer): if the cows, because they attached themselves to the service of the Mishkan, were given הויה and they are alive and enduring forever — Israel, who cleave to the Life of the Worlds, all the more so, as it is said (Deuteronomy 4:4): ״ואתם הדבקים בה׳ אלהיכם חיים כלכם היום״. This far is in the Midrash of Naso; and in the Midrash of Shir ha-Shirim Rabbah on the verse (Song of Songs 6:4): ״יפה את רעיתי כתרצה״. And what they said, that in the Eternal House they offered them, meaning that they remained alive until the time of Solomon — it seems to me that they also found support and allusion from the verse (I Kings 8:63): ״ויזבח שלמה את זבח השלמים אשר זבח לה׳ בקר עשרים ושנים אלף״ etc., for it attached the word הבקר to ה׳ יתעלה, since the verse should have said עשרים ושנים אלף בקר; and therefore it is fitting to grant them long life. Or this was a tradition in their hands. And Rabbi Meir came and added: He granted them eternal endurance because they attached themselves to the service of the Mishkan, and from them we draw a ק״ו (kal va-homer) to Israel, who cleave to הש״י. And there were among the wagons and the cattle ח״י in number. The two wagons he gave to the sons of Gershon according to their service, and the four wagons he gave to the sons of Merari according to their service, for the service of the sons of Gershon was lighter than the service of the sons of Merari, and therefore he gave the sons of Merari double, because the service of the sons of Gershon was light — and it consisted of the curtains, the coverings, and the screens — but the service of the sons of Merari was very heavy, as is explained in the verse and as is mentioned at the beginning of the parashah."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :register {:flags {1 ["the Eternal"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:30:34.739754438Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}